  "reason": "1) Evidence: The RNA encoder is the single most impactful design choice observed so far — pca_n2000 consistently outperforms scgpt by ~8-10% in accuracy (e.g., config_004 at 0.639 vs config_002 at 0.525 with identical image/aggregation/fusion settings). However, scvi_n2000, a variational autoencoder-based encoder, has not been tested in any completed experiment, leaving a major uncertainty about whether a learned generative representation can match or exceed the simple PCA baseline. 2) Question: config_009 (scvi_n2000, resnet50, avgpool, weighted) is a clean single-variable swap from the best-performing config_004 (pca_n2000, resnet50, avgpool, weighted), isolating the effect of the RNA encoder while holding image encoder, aggregation, and fusion constant. 3) Why this is the best next experiment: Under a limited budget, resolving whether a more expressive learned encoder can beat PCA is higher-value information than a small fusion-method comparison (config_021) or a profile-setting test that would require changing two variables simultaneously. If scvi_n2000 matches or beats pca_n2000, it opens a new promising direction; if it underperforms, it confirms that simple linear projections are sufficient and lets us focus remaining budget on aggregation/fusion/profile refinements around the PCA-based pipeline."
